  "authoritative_metadata": "manifest.json",
  "title": "Polynomial counting on Seymour's cubic bound",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "For every three-fold space H (a connected cubic triple system that is not 2-colourable, on Seymour's bound for minimal non-2-colourability) and every line L, the number of 2-colourings of H - L is computable in polynomial time, without any signing. It is twice the product of the amplitudes of the braces of B(H) (THREEFOLD-AMPLITUDE C): 1 for the two- and four-vertex braces, 5 for the Heawood graph, |det| of the line-face matrix for a planar brace (Berman), and for any other brace the trisum law at one square of its trisum tree, found by splitting along trisectors (Robertson-Seymour-Thomas). Each branch's leaning split is one weighted, row-restricted determinant of its planar leaf, in which the two branches beyond each other square weight the rows of that square's lines (Proposition 3; Lemma 2 gives one Pfaffian sign pattern per square). Two determinants per leaf. Checked against direct counts on 67 braces (16 to 80 vertices, up to 7 planar pieces, every root square, both colour classes), on seven three-fold spaces of 22 to 118 points with planar and trisum glue, and on omnitruncation trisums to 480 vertices; trisums of omnitruncated grids to 1720 vertices take seconds where variable elimination would need factors on 17 to 30 variables.",
